      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\nLooking at the provided code, I need to analyze the atomicity violation pattern <Read, Write, Write> for the global variable `glob`.\n\nLet me trace through the code:\n\n1. **Global variable**: `glob` (line 5)\n2. **Functions**:\n   - `svp_simple_513_001_main()`: main function (lines 13-23)\n   - `svp_simple_513_001_isr_1()`: ISR function (lines 7-11)\n\n3. **Operations on `glob`**:\n   - Line 9: `glob++` in ISR (compound operation: read then write)\n   - Line 19: `glob++` in main function (compound operation: read then write)\n\n4. **Decomposing compound operations**:\n   - Line 9: `glob++` → Read `glob`, then Write `glob = glob + 1`\n   - Line 19: `glob++` → Read `glob`, then Write `glob = glob + 1`\n\n5. **Priority analysis**:\n   - Main function has lower priority than ISR\n   - ISR can preempt main function execution\n\n6. **Atomicity violation detection**:\n   The pattern <Read, Write, Write> occurs when:\n   - **a1**: Read operation on `glob` by main function (line 19 - read part of `glob++`)\n   - **a2**: Write operation on `glob` by ISR (line 9 - write part of `glob++`)\n   - **a3**: Write operation on `glob` by main function (line 19 - write part of `glob++`)\n\n   The ISR can preempt the main function between the read and write operations of the compound `glob++` statement on line 19, causing the main function's write to be based on a stale value.\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"glob\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [19, 9, 19]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\nLooking at the code, I need to analyze the atomicity violation for the global variable `glob`.\n\n**Step 1: Check Control Conditions**\n\nLine 9 (ISR): `glob++` - No control conditions, always executable when ISR is triggered.\n\nLine 19 (main)